WEBVTT

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:07.563
The Delhi Metro carries five and a half million people a day, across 288 stations.

00:00:07.613 --> 00:00:10.450
At peak hours, its interchanges jam.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.762
Stations like Rajiv Chowk and Kashmere Gate become a daily crush.

00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:18.943
Keerthi spent four weeks, as a UX researcher,

00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:22.144
asking why, and what design could do about it.

00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:27.919
He started by looking at how other metros handle crowds and wayfinding:

00:00:27.919 --> 00:00:29.384
carriage maps in Tokyo,

00:00:29.384 --> 00:00:32.188
contactless gates, Hong Kong’s Octopus card,

00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:35.438
and in-train displays that tell you where to stand.

00:00:36.688 --> 00:00:39.920
He mapped four stakeholders: commuters, security,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.888
train operators, and the operating authority.

00:00:42.938 --> 00:00:45.252
Each has a different definition of a good day.

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:50.902
Then he surveyed 32 people, aged seventeen to forty-three.

00:00:50.952 --> 00:00:56.190
72 percent named peak-hour overcrowding as their biggest frustration.

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:01.190
42 percent said the official app lacks real-time crowd information.

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:05.202
And 95 percent of women said they feel unsafe.

00:01:06.052 --> 00:01:10.070
Two personas, Rahul and Priya, kept the work grounded:

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.344
two commuters, with very different mornings.

00:01:14.594 --> 00:01:20.657
To avoid falling for the first idea, every idea was judged from six directions,

00:01:20.657 --> 00:01:26.950
using Six Thinking Hats: facts, benefits, risks, feelings, new ideas, and process.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.325
Three proposals survived, judged on feasibility, impact and fit.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:34.717
A hybrid carriage system,

00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:38.637
where the first four carriages are pre-booked and the last two stay open.

00:01:38.687 --> 00:01:41.450
AR navigation inside stations.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.988
And smartcard top-up, right at the gate.

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:46.163
The app guides you in.

00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:50.011
Point your camera at a poster, follow the path to the right platform,

00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:53.258
and it tells you the best exit gate for where you’re going.

00:01:54.108 --> 00:01:57.425
The AR guidance includes a translator for signboards,

00:01:57.425 --> 00:02:00.680
and routes to lifts and wheelchair-accessible paths.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.255
The biggest insight: most of the crowd problem is an information problem.

00:02:06.305 --> 00:02:09.805
Rebuilding old stations is slow and expensive.

00:02:09.855 --> 00:02:14.166
Telling people which coach, which gate and which exit is cheap.

00:02:15.416 --> 00:02:17.966
This project stopped at low fidelity.

00:02:18.016 --> 00:02:22.776
The next step would be a pilot at one interchange, like Rajiv Chowk at peak,

00:02:22.776 --> 00:02:28.036
measuring how long it takes riders to reach the right platform, with and without it.

00:02:28.886 --> 00:02:31.402
Research and design by Keerthi Vardhan.

